Subject: [PATCH 1/5] feat(auth): multi-device Bearer tokens (v1.8.0, schema
 1.6.0)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Release blocker for the Android app.

Problem: the bridge kept ONE token per user in a single usermeta slot, so
verify-otp overwrote it on every login. Signing in on the phone silently
signed the user out of the web PWA and vice versa — and since subscriptions
are bought on the website, a user has to be signed in on both.

Tokens now live in wp_mrdastbaaf_api_tokens, one row per device:
  token_hash (sha256, never the raw secret) · device_id · device_name
  platform · app_version · issued_at · last_used_at · expires_at · revoked_at

- MAX_DEVICES = 3; a 4th login revokes the OLDEST session, never the newest.
- 30-day lifetime and the {user_id}.{secret} format are unchanged, so the
  shipped PWA 1.5.6 needs no update. verify-otp gains only optional params
  and three additive response keys (sessionId, expiresAt, expiresInSeconds).
- POST /auth/logout now revokes only the current session (or every session of
  a given device_id), instead of nuking the shared meta.
- New: GET /auth/sessions, DELETE /auth/sessions (all but current),
  DELETE /auth/sessions/{id}. Ownership is enforced in the SQL, so guessing
  another user's session id is a 404 rather than an IDOR.
- Fail-closed: if the token row cannot be inserted, verify-otp returns
  500 token_storage_failed instead of handing out a token that was never
  persisted.

Migration is lazy and lossless. Pre-upgrade tokens exist only as sha256 in
usermeta, which is exactly what token_hash stores — so the existing
constant-time comparison still validates them. The row is created on each
user's next authenticated request (an eager migration would be a full
usermeta scan, and most tokens belong to users who never return), then the
legacy meta is deleted. No user is logged out.

Operational:
- daily cron mrdastbaaf_tokens_prune removes revoked/expired rows after 90
  days; scheduled on activate/upgrade, cleared on deactivate.
- last_used_at is throttled to one write per session per 10 minutes. This
  plugin has a production incident on record caused by frequent writes to
  shared rows on shared hosting (v1.7.9-1.7.13); writing on every request
  would reintroduce it.

Tests: tests/php/test-token-store.php — 40 assertions covering parsing,
hashing, constant-time comparison, legacy compatibility, the fail-closed
rules in is_usable(), oldest-session selection with deterministic tie-break,
the 30-day expiry, and the touch throttle. Added tests/php/run-all.sh so the
suite is runnable on a bare PHP CLI.

Not covered by unit tests: issue/find_by_token/revoke/enforce_device_limit —
they need a live $wpdb. A staging checklist is in the doc.

Docs: docs/PLUGIN_v1.8.0_MULTI_DEVICE_TOKENS.md
