+# Plugin change — multi-device Bearer tokens
+
+**Version:** bridge `1.7.13` → `1.8.0` · **Schema:** `1.5.1` → `1.6.0`
+**Status:** release blocker for the Android app.
+
+---
+
+## Why
+
+Before this change the bridge kept **one token per user**, in a single usermeta
+slot (`mrdastbaaf_api_token`). `verify-otp` overwrote it on every login.
+
+The consequence is the problem: **signing in on the Android app silently signs
+the user out of the web PWA, and vice versa.** Subscriptions are purchased on the
+website — so a user has to be signed in on the web to buy, and on the phone to
+watch. With one slot, that is impossible.
+
+## What changed
+
+Tokens now live in a dedicated table, one row per device.
+
+```
+wp_mrdastbaaf_api_tokens
+  id · user_id · token_hash · device_id · device_name · platform · app_version
+  issued_at · last_used_at · expires_at · revoked_at
+```
+
+- **`token_hash`** — sha256 of the secret. The raw secret is never stored, so a
+  database leak does not yield a usable token.
+- **`device_id`** — a stable per-install identifier supplied by the client,
+  used for "sign out of this device".
+- **`revoked_at`** — soft revoke. Rows are kept 90 days for auditing ("when was
+  this device disconnected?") and then pruned by a daily cron.
+
+**Limits:** `MAX_DEVICES = 3` (phone + tablet + browser). When a fourth login
+arrives, the **oldest** session is revoked — never the newest, so a user is
+never logged out by their own login.
+
+**Lifetime is unchanged:** 30 days, no refresh token. On 401 the client
+re-authenticates with OTP, exactly as before.
+
+---
+
+## API contract
+
+### `POST /auth/verify-otp` — changed
+
+New **optional** request parameters:
+
+| Param | Type | Notes |
+|---|---|---|
+| `device_id` | string ≤64 | stable per-install id; enables "sign out of this device" |
+| `device_name` | string ≤100 | human label, e.g. `Samsung SM-A546` |
+| `platform` | string ≤20 | `android` \| `web` \| `ios` |
+| `app_version` | string ≤20 | e.g. `1.0.0` |
+
+Response — **existing keys unchanged**, three added:
+
+```jsonc
+{
+  "success": true,
+  "token": "42.9xK2mQ…",              // unchanged format: {user_id}.{secret}
+  "sessionId": "17",                   // NEW
+  "expiresAt": "2026-11-15T08:00:00+00:00",   // NEW, ISO 8601
+  "expiresInSeconds": 2592000,         // NEW
+  "user": { … }
+}
+```
+
+> **Backward compatible.** The web PWA 1.5.6 reads only `token` and `user` and
+> needs no change. Omitting the device parameters is fine — the session is simply
+> recorded as `device_id = ""`.
+
+A new error is possible: `500 token_storage_failed`. Fail-closed — the endpoint
+no longer returns a token that was never persisted.
+
+### `POST /auth/logout` — changed behaviour
+
+Now revokes **only the current session**. Other devices stay signed in, which is
+what a user expects from "log out".
+
+Optional body param `device_id` — revokes every session belonging to that device
+instead (for a client that wants "log out of this device" to be absolute).
+
+```jsonc
+{ "success": true, "message": "…", "revokedSessions": 1 }
+```
+
+### `GET /auth/sessions` — new
+
+Requires Bearer. Lists active sessions, newest first.
+
+```jsonc
+{
+  "sessions": [
+    {
+      "id": "17",
+      "deviceName": "Samsung SM-A546",
+      "platform": "android",
+      "appVersion": "1.0.0",
+      "createdAt": "2026-10-16 08:00:00",
+      "lastSeenAt": "2026-10-16 12:31:00",
+      "expiresAt": "2026-11-15 08:00:00",
+      "current": true
+    }
+  ],
+  "count": 1,
+  "maxDevices": 3
+}
+```
+
+No token, hash or secret ever appears in this response. `deviceName` falls back
+to a Persian label derived from `platform` when the client sent none.
+
+### `DELETE /auth/sessions` — new
+
+Revokes every session **except the current one**.
+
+```jsonc
+{ "success": true, "revokedSessions": 2, "message": "نشست‌های سایر دستگاه‌ها بسته شد." }
+```
+
+Returns `409 session_not_identified` when the current session cannot be
+determined — without that guard, "all except current" would silently become
+"all", logging the user out of the device they were using.
+
+### `DELETE /auth/sessions/{id}` — new
+
+Revokes one session. The ownership guard lives in the SQL
+(`WHERE id = ? AND user_id = ?`), so guessing another user's session id does
+nothing.
+
+```jsonc
+{ "success": true, "wasCurrent": false, "message": "نشست انتخاب‌شده بسته شد." }
+```
+
+Errors: `400 invalid_session`, `404 session_not_found`.
+
+---
+
+## Migration
+
+**No action required.** Existing tokens keep working.
+
+Old tokens exist only as `hash('sha256', secret)` in usermeta — the raw secret
+is not stored, and migration does not need it. The stored hash is copied into
+`token_hash`, so the existing constant-time comparison still succeeds.
+
+Migration is **lazy**, performed on each user's next successful authenticated
+request rather than in the upgrade block:
+
+- an eager migration would be a full-table scan over usermeta on a large site,
+- and most stored tokens belong to users who never come back.
+
+On the first successful legacy authentication the row is created with
+`device_id = 'legacy'`, `device_name = 'دستگاه پیشین'`, and the usermeta keys are
+deleted so the fallback path stops being used. **The user is not logged out.**
+
+`get_user_by_api_token()` therefore has two paths: the tokens table first, the
+legacy usermeta comparison as a fallback. Once every active user has migrated,
+the fallback becomes dead code and can be removed in a later release.
+
+## Housekeeping
+
+- **Daily cron** `mrdastbaaf_tokens_prune` deletes revoked/expired rows older
+  than 90 days. Scheduled on activation, on upgrade, and cleared on deactivation.
+- **`last_used_at` is throttled** to at most one write per session per 10
+  minutes. This plugin's history includes a production incident caused by
+  frequent writes to shared rows on shared hosting (v1.7.9–v1.7.13); a write on
+  every authenticated request would reintroduce exactly that risk.
+
+## Tests
+
+`tests/php/test-token-store.php` — 40 assertions, no WordPress required:
+
+```
+./tests/php/run-all.sh          # or: php tests/php/test-token-store.php
+```
+
+Covers token parsing (including a secret containing a dot), hashing, constant-time
+comparison, legacy-token compatibility, the fail-closed rules in `is_usable()`
+(revoked, expired, expiry-exactly-now, malformed), the oldest-session selection
+policy including deterministic tie-breaking, the 30-day expiry calculation, and
+the `last_used_at` throttle.
+
+The DB-touching paths (`issue`, `find_by_token`, `revoke*`, `enforce_device_limit`)
+are **not** covered by unit tests — they need a live `$wpdb`. They should be
+exercised on staging with the checklist below.
+
+## Staging checklist
+
+- [ ] Log in on the web PWA, then log in on Android → **both stay signed in**.
+- [ ] `GET /auth/sessions` from Android lists both, with `current: true` on the phone.
+- [ ] `POST /auth/logout` from Android → the web session still works.
+- [ ] Log in a 4th time → the oldest session is revoked, the new one works.
+- [ ] `DELETE /auth/sessions/{id}` for another user's id → 404, nothing revoked.
+- [ ] An existing pre-upgrade token still authenticates, and afterwards
+      `wp_usermeta` no longer contains `mrdastbaaf_api_token` for that user.
+- [ ] Confirm `wp_mrdastbaaf_api_tokens` exists with `user_active_idx`.
